# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the replay integration test.
#
# Only the ROS type support loader is suppressed here. Everything else stays under leak
# detection: a leak in the driver, in the bundled sbgECom or anywhere else in sbg_device is
# still reported and still fails the test.
#
# rosidl_typesupport_cpp resolves the type support library once per process, through
# rcpputils::SharedLibrary, and keeps the handle and its path alive for the lifetime of the
# process on purpose. There is no API to release them, so the blocks are still reachable at
# exit rather than lost, and creating a single rclcpp::Node is enough to allocate them:
#
#   Direct leak of 64 byte(s) in 1 object(s) allocated from:
#     #0  operator new(unsigned long)
#     #1  (/opt/ros/lyrical/lib/librosidl_typesupport_cpp.so+0x1a69)
#     #10 rcl_node_init
#     #11 rclcpp::node_interfaces::NodeBase::NodeBase(...)
#     #12 rclcpp::Node::Node(...)
#     #20 main src/main.cpp:8
#
#   Indirect leak of 80 byte(s) in 1 object(s) allocated from:
#     #1  rcutils_strndup
#     #2  rcutils_load_shared_library
#     #3  rcpputils::SharedLibrary::SharedLibrary(...)
#     #4  (/opt/ros/lyrical/lib/librosidl_typesupport_cpp.so+0x1a7b)
#
# Both entries below are taken from those stacks and nothing more.
leak:librosidl_typesupport_cpp
leak:rcutils_load_shared_library

# The launch_testing orchestrator is a plain python3 that this job does not audit: it only
# exists to start the driver, and none of this repository's C++ runs in it. Its runtime is
# instrumented solely because the ASan runtime has to be preloaded for it to load the
# instrumented rosidl extension modules at all. CPython keeps imported modules alive in
# sys.modules for the lifetime of the process, so its import machinery is reported at exit:
#
#   Direct leak of 76738 byte(s) in 32 object(s) allocated from:
#     #0  malloc
#     #1  (/usr/bin/python3.14+0x53d638)
#     #14 PyImport_ImportModuleLevelObject
#     #21 PyEval_EvalCode
#
# This entry silences allocations attributed to the interpreter binary. It does not affect the
# sbg_device process, which is audited separately and never has interpreter frames on its
# stacks, so leaks in the driver and in the bundled sbgECom are still reported.
leak:/usr/bin/python3
